Related to [feedback 11170483](https://developercommunity.visualstudio.com/t/Windows-Forms-CheckedListBox-DataSource-/11170483)

### .NET version

.NET 10

### Did it work in .NET Framework?

Yes

### Did it work in any of the earlier releases of .NET Core or .NET 5+?

_No response_

### Issue description

When a `CheckedListBox` is bound to a `List<T>`, `CheckedListBox.RefreshItems()` raises an `IndexOutOfRangeException` during the `DataSource` assignment. The exception is caught internally, so the application continues and the items are populated. However, if `DisplayMember` was assigned before `DataSource`, the setter's catch path clears `DisplayMember`, and the list displays each item's `ToString()` value instead of the selected property.

The relevant code is in [CheckedListBox.RefreshItems](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/514724aea49f8c17492c7b950f52bb777c0eebdf/src/System.Windows.Forms/System/Windows/Forms/Controls/ListBoxes/CheckedListBox.cs#L835-L851), [ListControl.DataSource](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/514724aea49f8c17492c7b950f52bb777c0eebdf/src/System.Windows.Forms/System/Windows/Forms/Controls/ListControl/ListControl.cs#L64-L80), and [ListBox.OnDataSourceChanged](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/514724aea49f8c17492c7b950f52bb777c0eebdf/src/System.Windows.Forms/System/Windows/Forms/Controls/ListBoxes/ListBox.cs#L1916-L1928). [Issue 6707](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/issues/6707) is related because it concerns this method and a reported .NET 6 `NullReferenceException`.



### Steps to reproduce

1. Create a WinForms application targeting .NET 9 or .NET 10.
2. Add a `CheckedListBox` to a form and assign `DisplayMember` before assigning its `DataSource`.
3. Bind it to a `List<TextList>` containing three items, where `TextList` has a `Name` property:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Windows.Forms;

var form = new Form();
var checkedListBox = new CheckedListBox();
form.Controls.Add(checkedListBox);
checkedListBox.DisplayMember = nameof(TextList.Name);
checkedListBox.DataSource = new List<TextList>
{
    new() { Name = "First" },
    new() { Name = "Second" },
    new() { Name = "Third" }
};

sealed class TextList
{
    public string Name { get; set; } = "";
}
```

4. Observe the first chance exception during the `DataSource` assignment. Repeating the binding with a list of a different item count also triggers it.

### Expected behavior

The data source should be bound without an exception, and the assigned `DisplayMember` should remain in effect.

### Actual behavior

`CheckedListBox.RefreshItems()` indexes the saved check state using the newly populated item count, although the saved state was sized using the previous `Items.Count`. This raises an `IndexOutOfRangeException`. The exception is swallowed, the application continues, and the items are populated, but `DisplayMember` is reset to an empty value when it was set before `DataSource`. Setting `DisplayMember` after `DataSource` displays the selected property correctly.

One possible fix is to limit the state restoration loop to the smaller of the saved state length and the current item count.


